**What**

`issue show` pipes the stored body through `acdc-parser` and a terminal
AsciiDoc converter. The body is stored verbatim and comes back byte-identical
from the object store, but the CLI offers no way to see it. Rendering is the
only read path, and there is no `--raw`. Nothing in the help, the prompts, or
any document tells a user the markup language is AsciiDoc.

**Where**

`crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:1281` -- `render_asciidoc_terminal`
`crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:1270` -- the only body output in `print_show_doc`
`crates/git-forge/Cargo.toml` -- `acdc-parser`, `acdc-converters-terminal`

**Reproduction**

Stored body, then `issue show` of the same issue:

    # Heading            ->  Heading            (marker silently eaten)
    **bold**             ->  bold               (markdown emphasis stripped)
    - bullet one         ->  * bullet one       (rewritten)
    blank lines          ->  collapsed
    trailing whitespace  ->  stripped
    fenced code block    ->  wrapped in two hardcoded 100-column rules

The 100-column rules are emitted regardless of terminal width and regardless of
whether stdout is a TTY.

**Why it matters**

Everyone arriving at this tool has GitHub muscle memory, and GitHub is
markdown. They will type markdown, the tool will accept it without complaint,
and `show` will quietly mangle it. Silent misinterpretation of user content is
worse than rejecting it. Filing these very issues, I had to guess -- I wrote
the review template in markdown because the instructions were markdown, and
`show` renders it as something else.

The absence of `--raw` is the sharper problem. If rendering is the only way to
read a body, then the body is effectively write-only for anyone who wants to
copy a command out of it, pipe it into a patch, or diff two versions. It also
contradicts the pitch: the data is not locked in, but the CLI locks it in
anyway.

**Options**

- Option A -- Add `--raw` (and default to raw when stdout is not a TTY, which
  is the grep-and-pipe convention `git` itself follows with color). Cheap,
  fixes the worst of it. Trade-off: none I can see.
- Option B -- Support both markup languages, detected per-record with a
  `content_type` field in the schema, defaulting to markdown because that is
  what people will type. Trade-off: two renderers to carry, and a schema change.
- Option C -- Commit to AsciiDoc and say so everywhere: in `issue new --help`,
  in the editor template that opens for an interactive body, and in the README.
  Trade-off: fights muscle memory forever, but it is at least honest and
  AsciiDoc is the better document language.

**Recommendation**

A immediately -- it is a flag and a TTY check, and it should land before the
talk regardless of anything else. Then C, with the editor pre-seeded with an
AsciiDoc comment line so the choice is discoverable at the moment it matters.
B is defensible and reasonable people will differ; I would avoid it because a
forge with two markup languages has a rendering bug report queue forever.